{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>you shall not defile your land</b> — According to our Rabbis, this is not a reason alone, namely \"so that you not defile your land,\" for if so it would be permitted outside the Land. Rather, it is a second negative prohibition. Now, one who hangs or even one who dies in the Land transgresses two negative prohibitions and one positive commandment, and outside the Land, one positive commandment and one negative prohibition, which is derived from the hanging, as I have explained. And from this negative prohibition Joshua buried the kings of Canaan on the very day, even though there is no element of the curse that our Rabbis mentioned regarding the blasphemer and the idolater in their hanging, but there was in them the matter of defilement of the land. Alternatively, he was concerned about the curse of Elohim, from the parable of the two brothers, as I have explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase 'derived from the hanging' (לָמֵד מִן הַתָּלוּי) refers back to the prohibition learned from the phrase about the hanged one, which Ramban explained earlier in his commentary on this passage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:52.036878225Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}